README
======

Dependencies
------------

This project needs to be run from within the following Python virtual environment;

  > /home/craig/local/source_code/python/sine_wave/venv/bin/python

The reason for this is because it relies on the following Python packages;

  - numpy
  - matplotlib
  - numpy-quaternion


Running the project
-------------------

Following is an example of how to invoke this package;

  python3 ./quaternion_rotation_over_unit_sphere.py False rotation 0.577350 0.577350 0.577350 720 1 0 0


Help message
------------

Manipulate a 3d vector in one of three ways using a quaternion.

Usage:

  vectorQuaternionRotate verbose operation_type vector_x vector_y vector_z rotationAngle axis_x axis_y axis_z

where:

  verbose       : utility runs in a verbose fashion
                - Haskell type : Bool
                - [True | False]
  operation     : partially multiply, fully multiply, or rotate the 3d vector by the rotation quaternion
                - Haskell type : String
                - [partial_multiplication | full_multiplication | rotation]
  axis_x        : the x component of the 3d vector which acts as the axis of rotation
                - Haskell type : Double
  axis_y        : the y component of the 3d vector which acts as the axis of rotation
                - Haskell type : Double
  axis_z        : the z component of the 3d vector which acts as the axis of rotation
                - Haskell type : Double
  rotationAngle : the angle in degrees by which the 3d vector is to be rotated
                - Haskell type : Double
                - Measured in degrees
  vector_x      : the x component of the 3d vector that is to be rotated
                - Haskell type : Double
  vector_y      : the y component of the 3d vector that is to be rotated
                - Haskell type : Double
  vector_z      : the z component of the 3d vector that is to be rotated
                - Haskell type : Double

Example:

  vectorQuaternionRotate False rotation 1 0 0 90 0 0 1

Rotate the 3d vector <0,0,1> by 90 degrees, i.e. pi/2 radians, around the axis which is represented by the
vector <1,0,0>.

> Notes on the operation of this utility
  ===========================

If the vector which denotes the axis of rotation, is not a unit vector, then this utility will convert it
into one first before using it in the calculations.

This utility will not do the same however with the vector that is to be rotated. This is mentioned, because
if this vector is too long, then it won't be displayed properly in the resulting plot.
